    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Do you often feel like you have a 'motor' inside you that keeps you constantly active, or do you find it hard to sit still for long periods of time?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S010",
      "patient_response": "Um, I don't know if I'd call it a motor, exactly. Like, I'm not running laps around the office or anything. But I definitely have a hard time just sitting completely still. Like, whenever I have to sit through those really long training classes at work—we actually just had a three-hour one for this new scheduling software—I always notice myself clicking my pen or bouncing my foot the whole time. I'll just be shifting around in my chair constantly, even when I'm really trying to sit perfectly still and pay attention to the presenter. \n\nActually, speaking of pens, I was using my absolute favorite one during that training. It's one of those gel ones, but it has to be the ultra-fine point because the medium ones smudge everywhere. Especially on that weird, glossy paper they always use to print the training manuals. My boss always orders the absolute cheapest bulk pens for the supply closet, the ones that barely even write and just scratch the paper, you know? So I started buying my own and hiding them in my bottom desk drawer. But Brenda, the receptionist, she's always coming over and \"borrowing\" them and I literally never see them again. It drives me crazy because they aren't cheap. So now I have a little lockbox in there... \n\nUm, wait. Sorry. Did that answer your question? I feel like I totally just started rambling about office supplies. What did you ask me again?",
